    "updated_at": "2016-01-28T22:20:00Z",
    "body": "All really good points. My thoughts:\n\n> 1. As you have no doubt noticed, the audio/video tracks APIs are not widely implemented yet.\n> 2. With adaptive streaming, there is conceptually still only one video track. Presumably it's possible to have multi-angle video also with HLS or DASH, so there would have to be some way of grouping together video tracks that are conceptually the same.\n\nIf I were designing this in a vacuum by myself, I would be inclined to represent each quality level for the same video as a separate VideoTrack with kind `main` and leave it up to individual video formats to find some way to distinguish the variants through the `label` property. As a side benefit, this would allow application developers to introspect on the quality level currently being displayed, which is a common desire for analytics. Maybe the extra functionality would help shift the value proposition for Video/AudioTrack implementors. Alternate angles could continue using the `alternative` kind.\n\n> Is this something you want for video.js, and is it for mobile browsers that support HLS? \n\nYes, I'm primarily asking for video.js but the scope is DASH and HLS on desktop and mobile. We'd like to support alternate video and audio tracks all over the place so we're looking to polyfill the TrackList APIs where they aren't present. Bitrate selection/introspection is another cross-format player feature and we'd rather expose that through a standardized API (that may or may not get implemented natively in browsers) than come up with a video.js-specific way of doing things.\n\n> I assume that you have investigated using Media Source Extensions to implement HLS or DASH in the browser, but that the same-origin policy ruins it for you? If not, that would really allow all the flexibility you want.\n\nActually, it seems to me most developers would be willing to fight epic battles with CORS and CDNs if they can get rid of Flash. We definitely have the flexibility, we'd just like to continue our design philosophy of polyfilling advanced HTMLMediaElement features rather than inventing our own. Filing this issue was a bit of an experiment-- thanks for taking the time to consider it.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
