    "updated_at": "2016-02-02T20:19:42Z",
    "body": "Aggregating the different variants onto a single track sounds reasonable to me but I think there will be more factors than bitrate to consider. Selecting variants based on resolution is common as well, for instance. I was thinking that info could be packed into `VideoTracks.label` in format-specific ways. You could adapt that strategy onto a single VideoTrack, though:\n\n``` js\npartial interface VideoTrack {\n  sequence<string> getAvailableRepresentations();\n  // promise resolves when change has taken effect\n  Promise<void> setPreferredRendition(string representation);\n};\n```\n\nThe term \"Representation\" comes from the DASH world. HLS would probably refer to alternative streams of the same track as \"variants\".\n\nI'm not an expert on Flash-based adaptive switching but I believe you'd use [NetStream.play2(...)](http://help.adobe.com/en_US/FlashPlatform/reference/actionscript/3/flash/net/NetStream.html#play2%28%29) to switch quality levels during playback. Each quality level has a name. The player switches quality levels by calling play2() and passing in the name of the stream they wish to use and how soon they'd like the switch to occur. It is the application's job to provide the list of available quality levels-- Flash just provides a seamless way to switch between them. In HTML terms, I think it would be like allowing javascript developers to specify a list of URLs for the VideoTrack and then having a method to choose between those URLs during playback. That doesn't seem very compatible with the current implementations of manifest-based formats like DASH and HLS.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
